Janis Papanagnou wrote:
  [...]
> Even as a non-native speaker I know that there's some relevant difference
> in "instantly" and "quite instantly".

As a native speaker, this is actually a jolly interesting one. "Quite"
in phrases like "quite good" means, roughly, "a bit", but when used with
a more absolute word, like "perfect" or "impossible" or "instantly",
it's an intensifier meaning "completely" or "precisely". If anything,
"quite instantly" carries a greater sense of total and utter
instantaneousness than just "instantly".

(I wouldn't expect a non-native speaker to know that, and I don't think
it adds anything to the argument; I just think it's quite interesting
:-)

